{"text":"For PyTorch’s h_t=z_t h_{t−1}+(1−z_t)n_t, chrono initialization maps to effective update bias b_z,j=log(T_j−1), giving bias-only retention z_j=1−1/T_j. The original prescription samples T_j−1 uniformly over [1,T_max−1]. The negative bias belongs to the complementary write gate, already represented by 1−z; it does not imply b_r=−b_z. The reset gate controls candidate computation, so leaving its bias at zero is a separate implementation choice. [Tallec and Ollivier](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.11188), [PyTorch GRU equations](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.15/generated/torch.nn.GRU.html).

With nonzero gate weights, z_t=σ(W_xz x_t+W_hz h_{t−1}+b_z); therefore log(T−1) specifies a nominal initial retention, not an enforced memory lifetime. Even centered preactivations do not guarantee E[z_t]=σ(b_z). Candidate recurrence and gate derivatives also affect the full state Jacobian. For constant z and an isolated carry path, the exact e-folding time is −1/log(z), approximately T for large T. PyTorch adds input and recurrent update biases: their sum must equal log(T−1), rather than assigning that value to both. These are deductions from the documented equations.

A minimal proposed control replaces heterogeneous initial biases with one common b*=logit(mean_j σ(b_z,j)), matching mean bias-only retention while preserving weights, reset initialization, architecture and training settings. This tests heterogeneous initialization against a general retention shift; learned gates remain dynamic. To isolate fixed coordinate timescales more directly, compare frozen heterogeneous z_j against frozen uniform z*=mean_j z_j, disabling update-gate input/recurrent contributions in both. Candidate/reset networks remain jointly trained within each single GRU model."}
